Greg Menke <gregm-news@toadmail.com> writes:

>"Uli Link" <Ulrich--nO--(dot)-sPAM--Link@Epost.de> writes:

>> 
>> Don't agree: the optimization of the GCC has become much better:
>> in 9 out of 10 times the GCC produces faster binaries than vendor compilers
>> Perhaps not, if you will tune every single object, and put a lot of #pragma
>> in your source to keep the vendor compilers from producing invalid code.
>> On AIX IBM's xlc build faster common-mode bins, but if you mind peak
>> performance, you should try the GCC 3.x and the cpu-specific optimizations.
>> Never got working binaries from any GNU code with xlc and "-O5".
>> GCC's results were up to 10% faster on PowerPC, with greater difference for
>> float compared to integer.
>> Same for Solaris x86, SCO and NCR's MP-RAS.
>> A great time-saver of the GCC is the fix of bugs in the O/S include headers.

>I have never yet seen gcc 3.2 or previous generate faster code than
>Sun's C compiler.  I've seen improvements up to 10* by switching to
>Sun's cc, depending on the code.  Thats with specific cpu/architecture
>selection and high optimization selected for both gcc and cc,
>compiling plain C code.


When people are producing better code using gcc than vendor compilers,
its because 1) they don't know the options for the vendor compiler too
well, or 2) they have a program whose execution time is dominated by one
or two loops and gcc happens to handle the particular way that code is
written better or 3) they aren't using some of the higher end optimization
features like interprocedural optimization, profile based optimization,
etc.  Often just reording the C code or changing arrays to pointers or
vice versa, etc. can change the performance significantly.  Certain code
constructs just fit what the compiler expects to see for given optimizations
better than others.  Those who experiment reap the benefits.

Look at the recent controversy over Apple's G5 ads where they used gcc
for both the G5 and P4.  The P4 took at least a 30% hit by using gcc,
because it can't come close to Intel's compiler in optimization.  I'm
pretty sure once IBM releases their own hardware based on the 970 it'll
show numbers much better than Apple's for equivalent hardware, because
IBM's compiler is better, at least when you use all the optimizations
that are available to you.

Whether the numbers you see at SPEC are realistic is another matter,
most people (and many commercial apps) just use -O or -O<big number>
rather than checking what options are out there.  gcc tends to be
relatively better in these limited circumstances (mainly because it
rolls a lot more stuff in the -O and -O2 than vendor compilers for
code quality/safety reasons)  Profile based optimization is a pain,
one of my major pet peeves with SPEC is that it allows this for the
'base' numbers.

