> It's not always as simple as it might seem on the first look...
> 
> Benjamin

Probably not that complicated either - for Sun with Solaris.
Everything is in place.

For the other major commercial UNIX operating systems (AIX, HP-UX,
IRIX) I accept the costs would be higher in the short term for the
vendors, as they would have to 

1) Write a licence allowing the non-commercial use.
2) Get that license approved by their lawyers.
3) Perhaps put additional servers in place.
4) Maintain a web site 
and I expect a few other things too. 

But in the longer term, I still maintain it would be good for those
companies to allow their products to be more widely known. 

At the end of the day, all I'm really asking for is the ability to
*legally* use a piece of commercial software (which uses a lot of free
software) for  non-commercial use. That software will only be of use
to me if used on a computer sold at a profit by the vendor in the
first place. So I'm not going to use Sun's Solaris on a computer that
HP made a profit on. 

I don't suppose making Solaris, IRIX, AIX, HP-UX free for
non-commercial use would exactly please Microsoft. And that only has
to be a good thing! 

