In article <24819cc3.0307210732.5f01a8ef@posting.google.com>,
Schmuck <yamahasw40@latinmail.com> wrote:
> What were the options for filesystems under Dec OSF/1? Can modern
> implementations of Alpha Tru64 Unix mount a filesystems created under
> OSF/1 and vice versa?

You can create and mount UFS (the Berkeley fast file system that OSF/1
used) file systems under Tru64, but I'm not sure why you need to do this
to recover a tape. The user API and file system semantics for all UNIX
native file systems are similar, and for this case they're close enough
to identical that it shouldn't matter which file system you restore to.

Assuming you can read the tapes at all, there should be no file system
related issues keeping you from rebuilding the directory trees and files
and backing them up again. I would recommend using an archive format like
CPIO or TAR rather than a backup format for your new tapes, though, to
avoid compatibility issues in the future.

