In article <3f24087a_2@news.arcor-ip.de>,
	"Thomas Dehn" <thomas-usenet@arcor.de> writes:
> In Germany [...]
> The vendor cannot impose any restrictions at
> all after the user has purchased the software,
> especially, license agreements which pop up
> during installation (i.e., when the software has already been
> bought) are completely void.

Same is true in the UK. The only licence conditions which can
be held up are those you were made aware of before purchase.
In terms of microsoft software, this is normally held to be
what's written on the outside of the box and can be read before
breaking the seal. Microsoft has lost court cases in the UK
trying to impose conditions other than those printed on the
outside of the box.

Something else software vendors have to be careful of in the
UK are time restricted licenses which are enforced in the
software. If the enforcement results in the customer being
unable to access their data, they can sue the supplier for
consequential damages. In the case of St. Albans city council
verses ICL, the consequential damages awarded against ICL
(Fujitsu) for one such case were �9 million. Time enforced
licences are still legal in the UK, but must be implemented
such that after the time expiry, the customer can still
retrieve all their data from the application. The time
expiry could be implemented such that no new data could be
added nor existing data further edited, for example.

-- 
Andrew Gabriel


