In article <9a782df9.0307160856.5a2a1388@posting.google.com>,
Manore <M.Orellana@vodafone.es> wrote:
> I have a problem in my alphaserver running Tru64 V4.0F, when i start
> the system i found the message: "bad super block: magic number wrong",

This message can be caused by a number of things, including a bad drive
or a bad device file: if it reads a block that isn't a superblock it will
fail as well.

Make sure that /dev/rrz16a is good. fsck may be opening that and finding the
wrong volume.

Make sure there really IS a file system there. newfs -N just prints out what
it would do to create a file system, it doesn't care what's actually on the
disk.

Check it with other filesystem tools like tunefs and dumpfs.

