yamahasw40@latinmail.com (Schmuck) wrote in message news:<24819cc3.0307171340.ffd452d@posting.google.com>...
> There is a lot of FUD coming out of various vendors about the future
> of HPs "other OSs". Ie.. if its not HP-UX, it will die with the switch
> to Itanium.

I'll assume you're not worried here about survival of Linux or
Windows, but about Tru64, OpenVMS, NonStop, and MPE/iX.

MPE/iX has an announced end-of-life plan, with various migration
options.  Tru64 is being merged into HP-UX (AdvFS and TruClusters are
already running on HP-UX 11i in HP's labs), with Tru64 support
continuing in parallel for a number of years yet.  OpenVMS is being
ported to Itanium and plans are for that to continue on indefinitely,
with the first release on Itanium (V8.0) just going out recently. 
NonStop is also being ported to Itanium, resuming the original
direction (before Compaq temporarily detoured it toward Alpha) with
2004 as the target date there.

> What is the real deal? If I buy an Alphaserver today will I be dealing
> with HP Techs who can't even spell Tru64?

Since HP bought Compaq, who had bought Digital, and employment of
techs in general continued under the new management, you'd typically
be dealing with the same techs who knew and supported Alpha before. 
Alpha hardware support is committed for a minimum of 5 years after
last-sale, which looks to be at least 5 years after 2006, or 2011 at
this point, and could quite possibly be longer.

> Essentially we need to consider the TCO of the shop before we
> recommend a platform. (I don't actually care that much.. Anybody but
> Microsoft). If these guys will have an uphill battle with support over
> the time-life of this sistem (predicted at 5 years with a possible
> stretch to 7), they are better off retraining their OSF and VMS guys
> upfront.

While there are some differences between Tru64 and HP-UX, particularly
in the area of system administration, that transition is not seen as
particularly difficult, and material is available on HP's website
about the differences.

There's no need to re-train their VMS guys, as VMS on Itanium will be
the same thing they're already familiar with.  Moving to Itanium is
likely to be easier than the VAX-to-Alpha (32-bit to 64-bit)
transition they've likely already gone through.  And since Alpha
support will continue, they can do that transition on their own
timeframe.


